Archives Pipeline Installation

1. Clone the GitHub repository or download the following files:
   - GUI.py
   - pipeline.py
   - files.csv.xslt
   - files.html.xslt

2. Make sure Python is installed.

3. Install the required Python packages:

   py -m pip install lxml
   py -m pip install bagit

4. Open the project folder in VS Code.

5. Run the pipeline GUI:

   py GUI.py

Notes:
- GUI.py, pipeline.py, and both XSLT files should remain in the same folder.
- BagIt is optional, but it is required if you want to use the BagIt packaging step.
- If the `python` command does not work on Windows, use `py` instead.

Optional file-format identification (required for PUIDs and identified MIME types):

The Pipeline still transfers files and builds XML in Python. FITS supplies
optional identification metadata; DataAccessioner itself is not launched.
Nothing downloads or installs FITS automatically.

The repository includes FITS 1.6.0 at tools/fits-1.6.0. Keep that directory
inside the tools folder next to pipeline.py, as supplied by the repository. With Java
available, launch the GUI normally; FITS_HOME is not required. Discovery is
relative to pipeline.py, so launching from another working directory also works.
Java is still a separate prerequisite; it is not bundled.

For a complete repository checkout, follow step 1 below, then run `py GUI.py`.
Steps 2-4 describe an optional external FITS installation/override. To run the
direct diagnostic commands in steps 5-6 against the bundled copy, first set
`$env:FITS_HOME = (Resolve-Path .\tools\fits-1.6.0).Path` from the repository.
This variable is for those diagnostic commands, not required by normal GUI runs.

Windows setup

1. Check Java in PowerShell:

   Get-Command java
   java -version

   Current FITS requires Java 11 or later. If Java is missing or older, install
   a supported 64-bit Java runtime/JDK, for example Eclipse Temurin 17 (LTS)
   for Windows from https://adoptium.net/temurin/releases/?version=17
   Use its MSI installer and enable the PATH and JAVA_HOME options. Then open
   a new PowerShell and repeat the checks above. Check the chosen FITS release
   notes for any newer requirements. No Java reinstall is needed if your
   existing supported runtime already works with FITS.

   Pipeline uses JAVA_HOME\bin\java.exe when JAVA_HOME is set, otherwise Java
   on PATH. A stale JAVA_HOME can therefore override a working PATH installation.
   If JAVA_HOME is set, verify that runtime too:

   if ($env:JAVA_HOME) { & "$env:JAVA_HOME\bin\java.exe" -version }

   Sources: https://github.com/fitstool/fits#system-requirements
            https://adoptium.net/installation/windows

2. Download the packaged FITS release ZIP from:
   https://github.com/fitstool/fits/releases

   Choose the fits-<version>.zip distribution asset, NOT GitHub's "Source code"
   ZIP. Extract the complete distribution to a stable writable directory, e.g.
   C:\Tools\fits, or a tools folder under your user profile. FITS may write logs
   in its installation directory, so choose a location you can write to.
   Keep fits.bat, lib, xml, tools, and all other distribution files together.
   If extraction creates an inner fits-<version> folder, use that inner folder
   as FITS_HOME. FITS_HOME must contain fits.bat directly.

3. Set FITS_HOME for the current PowerShell session. Replace this example path
   with your extracted distribution root:

   $env:FITS_HOME = "C:\Tools\fits"
   $env:FITS_HOME
   Test-Path -LiteralPath $env:FITS_HOME -PathType Container
   Test-Path "$env:FITS_HOME\fits.bat" -PathType Leaf
   Get-ChildItem "$env:FITS_HOME\lib\*.jar" | Select-Object -First 3

   Both Test-Path checks must return True, and the last command must list JARs.
   If the variable might be unset, check it BEFORE passing it to Test-Path:

   if ($env:FITS_HOME) { Test-Path -LiteralPath $env:FITS_HOME } else { 'FITS_HOME is unset' }

4. Persist the working path for your Windows user (no administrator required):

   [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('FITS_HOME', $env:FITS_HOME, 'User')
   [Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable('FITS_HOME', 'User')

   This does not update already-running programs. Fully close/reopen VS Code
   and PowerShell before using a newly persisted variable. Alternatively, launch
   `py GUI.py` from the same PowerShell where step 3 set $env:FITS_HOME.

5. Test FITS directly with an existing local PDF (use copies for testing).
   Replace the sample path below with an actual file:

   $sampleFile = (Resolve-Path "C:\path\to\sample.pdf").Path
   $fitsReport = Join-Path $env:TEMP 'pipeline-fits-sample.xml'
   Push-Location $env:FITS_HOME
   try {
       & .\fits.bat -i $sampleFile -o $fitsReport
       $fitsExit = $LASTEXITCODE
   } finally { Pop-Location }
   $fitsExit
   if ($fitsExit -ne 0) { throw 'FITS failed; inspect console output and fits.log in FITS_HOME' }
   Select-String -Path $fitsReport -Pattern 'identity|externalIdentifier|fmt/'

   Look for an identity, MIME type, and a DROID externalIdentifier/PUID.
   A specific PDF version may have a different PUID than fmt/18; use the value
   actually reported. If FITS identifies formats but no PUID, inspect DROID's
   output/configuration and PRONOM signatures in the FITS distribution. Preserve
   the release's default tool configuration initially; do not invent a PUID.
   See https://github.com/fitstool/fits and the selected release's documentation.

6. Run a real end-to-end test from the Pipeline repository:

   $env:PIPELINE_TEST_FITS_HOME = $env:FITS_HOME
   .venv\Scripts\python.exe -m unittest discover -s tests -v

   Use `py` instead of `.venv\Scripts\python.exe` if not using this virtualenv.
   The opt-in test generates a valid PDF 1.4 with a .bin extension and requires
   real FITS to identify fmt/18 in XML, HTML and CSV. It also verifies MIME,
   version, MD5, size, UUID and transferred contents. It cleans up its temp data.

7. Test an actual accession through the GUI from the same configured shell:

   py GUI.py

   Choose a small input folder containing a copy of the sample PDF, a separate
   output folder, a new accession number such as FITS-TEST, and copy mode.
   For an initial identification test leave optional bagging off. After completion,
   set $reportRoot to the selected output folder and inspect the saved reports:

   $reportRoot = "C:\path\to\output"
   Select-String -Path "$reportRoot\FITS-TEST.xml" -Pattern 'formatName|formatRegistryKey|fmt/'
   Select-String -Path "$reportRoot\FITS-TEST_files.html" -Pattern 'fmt/'
   Import-Csv "$reportRoot\FITS-TEST_files.csv" | Select-Object 'file name','file format','format version','format registry key/PUID','MIME Type'
   Select-String -Path "$reportRoot\pipeline_log_FITS-TEST_*.log" -Pattern '\[FITS:'

   The PUID should agree with direct FITS output for the same file/configuration.
   Existing XML reports are not retroactively enriched; create a fresh accession.

Configuration and log reference

- Precedence: Python fits_home argument, then FITS_HOME, then tools/fits-1.6.0
  relative to pipeline.py, then fits.bat (Windows) or fits.sh (other systems)
  found on PATH. No recursive directory discovery.
  An invalid explicit path is reported instead of silently using another install.
  An existing bundled directory with missing launcher/libraries also produces a
  nonfatal configuration warning rather than silently switching installations.
- The Pipeline checks <FITS_HOME>\fits.bat (or fits.sh) as a distribution marker,
  checks lib/*.jar, and runs Java -version once per accession. It then invokes
  Java with classpath <FITS_HOME>/lib/* and edu.harvard.hul.ois.fits.Fits directly,
  without a shell. It does not execute or inherit custom edits to fits.bat/fits.sh.
- One global warning: NOT_CONFIGURED, INVALID_HOME, LAUNCHER_MISSING,
  LIBRARIES_MISSING, or JAVA_UNAVAILABLE. Identification is skipped for the
  entire accession; transfer and basic PREMIS metadata continue.
- Per-file log status: TIMEOUT (300 seconds), EXIT_ERROR (exit code and last
  2000 diagnostic characters), INVALID_XML, or EXECUTION_ERROR (e.g. missing
  report/I/O failure). Format metadata is omitted for that file; transfer continues.
- READY records the selected installation and Java runtime. IDENTIFIED records
  format count and PUIDs (or "none reported"). NO_IDENTIFICATION means valid FITS
  XML had no useful format identity; its raw XML is retained. These use INFO level,
  which the GUI already enables in pipeline_log_<accession>_<timestamp>.log.
- UUID, MD5, size and fixity do not depend on FITS. PUIDs are included only when
  FITS supplies PRONOM identifiers. Unknown files may legitimately have no PUID.
- FITS adds processing time. Pre-existing transfer/I/O errors can still stop a
  run; identification/configuration failures are nonfatal.
